glimt: the public page and its API

Task 323, PRD 019 §0/§7/§8.

Served by this service on hej.nathejk.dk rather than exported into the marketing site.
An export would mean a second copy of the photographs, a second retention window to keep
correct, and a hidden glimt that stays up somewhere else. One store, one takedown.

Every media item gets its own img tag. That is the maintainer's desktop requirement -
media must be reachable without a swipe - and the app's fix cannot carry here, because
prev/next buttons on an Embla carousel need a bundle this page deliberately does not load.
So a four-photograph glimt is four tags in an auto-fit grid: side by side where there is
room, stacked where there is not. No script, no scroll-snap, and a test that asserts both
are absent.

Trap 2 turned out to be answerable structurally, which is much better than "the handlers
ignore the cookie". requireAuth is the only place a session enters the request context, so
a bare handler does not choose not to read one -- contextGetSession returns false because
nothing put a session there. The registration is the guarantee. Guarded from three
directions, since that rests on an assumption a future change could break quietly: an AST
test that no public route is wrapped and that no handler in the chain touches the session
or app.sessions, a behavioural test that the JSON and the HTML are byte-identical with and
without a real member cookie, and the annotation guard now failing a 401 documented on a
public route.

404 rather than 403 for a non-public glimt: the authenticated route answers 403 on purpose,
because there the caller is a known member and an honest refusal tells them the photo was
not shared with them, whereas here a 403 would confirm to an anonymous caller that a glimt
with this id exists and is not public.

publiclyVisible re-checks the audience on the media route, which fetches one glimt by id
rather than going through PublicFeed. Without it an id alone would be an unauthenticated
read of a group-scoped photograph of a child, so it is tested with a valid member session
attached -- the case a reader would assume is fine.

streamGlimtMedia now takes the cache-control value as a parameter rather than the public
route wrapping the ResponseWriter to overwrite it. The authenticated route's private is a
privacy property and belongs at its own call site, not somewhere a reader has to prove
nobody downstream changed. There is a test that it stayed private.

The anonymous reporter is recorded as a sentinel, not an IP. glimt_report is keyed by
reporter so it needs a value, and recording the address would put a personal identifier of
the one participant in this feature who never agreed to anything into an append-only audit
table, to solve a duplicate-counting problem that does not matter. Several anonymous
reports of one glimt collapse into a row; it is hidden on the first anyway.

The service worker denylist entry was already present but undocumented, so it read as
arbitrary. It deserves a test because it fails asymmetrically: a parent following a shared
link has no service worker and gets the real page, so the bug is invisible to everyone who
would naturally check it, and the only people who see it are installed members getting the
app shell. The spec asserts it in the config and in the built sw.js, which are different
claims.

Two new by-IP limiters, separate from the member-keyed ones because an IP is a much worse
key -- a school behind one NAT shares a budget -- so the public read ceiling has to be far
more generous, and mixing them would drag the member limit down.

publicHoldLabel duplicates the frontend's attributionLine, named as duplication in the
code because there is nothing to share it through.

Not opened in a browser, and nothing links to the page yet from anywhere -- where a public
link belongs is a decision for whoever owns the site.
